Probability is connected to experiment by repeated trials. Suppose the same experiment is performed $N_{\mathrm{trials}}$ times, and let $N(i)$ be the number of trials that produce outcome $i$. Then the frequency interpretation is $p_i=
 \lim_{N_{\mathrm{trials}}\to\infty}
 \frac{N(i)}{N_{\mathrm{trials}}}.$ This is a physical hypothesis expressed through an ideal limit. No laboratory performs infinitely many experiments. The claim is that sufficiently many trials make the ratio settle close enough to $p_i$ for the intended prediction. Finite samples fluctuate around the limiting value.

